// A spawned thread that dies before it sends used to leave its reader blocked in `receive` for ever:
// no output, no exit, and the thread's own error already printed above with nothing to connect it to
// the silence. The test is the one Go uses -- if every live thread is blocked on a channel then
// nothing can ever send, so no blocked thread can ever wake -- and it is reported rather than waited
// out. Note the order of the two errors: the worker's failure is printed first, then the reason main
// cannot continue, which is the pair a reader needs.
fn divide(n: int) -> int {
    return 100 / n
}

fn worker(out: Channel<int>) {
    send(out, divide(0))
}

fn main() {
    val ch: Channel<int> = channel_new()
    spawn worker(ch)
    printf("received {}\n", receive(ch))
}
